				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607673413" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1607664526 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Dec 2020 05:28</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-891087/help:questions-desk">Writing Help / Help: Questions Desk</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13905791/help:addendum-usage">Help: Addendum usage?</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4842017">
									

<blockquote>
<p>For example its creators, where the Foundation found it, what attracted the Foundation to it, etc. Would that go in addendum or somewhere else like a log sort of?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Depends on how key the backstory is to understanding the basic anomaly. If it's difficult to understand the absolute basics of what the anomaly does without the backstory, put the backstory in the description.</p>
<p>One definition of &quot;addendum&quot; is &quot;an item of additional material, typically omissions, added at the end of a book or other publication.&quot; Addenda are additions to the primary description content. Since Foundation discovery tends to not be crucial to basic description of an anomaly, that content usually is put in addenda.</p>
<p>So far as logs go, it depends on the content in said logs. I typically encourage logs only if the format is conducive to storytelling; if you have information that is well-presented in a short paragraph, there's no need to have an entire drawn-out log just for the sake of a log.</p>

							</div>

								
					
